WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:11.380
Hello everyone, welcome to the Langfocus channel
and my name is Paul

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:14.820
Today I'm going to talk about 
a language called Hindustani

00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.580
Or it's sometimes called Hindustani

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.940
 

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:25.180
There are countries with names like Pakistan, Afghanistan, Kazakhstan...
Is there a country called Hindustan?

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:31.580
Well, kind of. At certain points throughout history, 
the term Hindustan has been used to refer all of India.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.480
But more generally, it is used to refer to the area
of the Northern Indian subcontinent.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.960
The term Hindustan itself is a Persian term 
meaning Land of the Indus (River)

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:44.360
Hindustani is a language that arose in the Hindustan region.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.080
Maybe you've never heard of Hindustani before, 
but you've probably heard of Hindi &amp; Urdu.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:55.880
Hindustani is a pluricentric language, meaning that it's 
a single language that has 2 different standard varieties.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.020
 

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:03.460
Even though Hindi and Urdu are refered to by different names,
the spoken language is essentially the same,

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.760
aside from some differences in accent
 and maybe local, regional vocabulary.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:11.920
The standard languages however, which are used 
in writing and in formal situations,

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:17.560
have more significant differences that reflect 
the cultural and religious identity of the speakers.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.740
But the development of these 2 standard languages 
is a recent phnomenon.

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:27.040
For most of its history, Hindustani was a single language 
that was refered to by various different names.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:31.380
Hindustani is spoken as a native language 
by lots and lots of people.

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:37.820
It depends on how you count it but around 324 million people are native speakers of either Hindi or Urdu.

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:48.600
There are 258 million speakers of Hindi in India
and about 52 million speakers of Urdu in India.
And 14.7 million speakers of Urdu in Pakistan.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.600
And that's refering to native speakers.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.740
They also functions as lingua franca 
in very linguistically diverse regions.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:59.440
There are an additional 214 million
second language speakers of Hindustani.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:05.120
This is perhaps most striking in Pakistan, where only
8% of the people speaks Urdu as a native language.

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:11.440
But over 94 million people speak it as a second language 
because it is widely learned as the language of education.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.820
There are also 120 million second language
 speakers of Hindi in India.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:20.760
So if we include both native speakers and second language speakers of both varieties of Hindustani,

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:24.980
then there are about 538 million speakers of the language.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.560
Hindustani a member of the Indo-Aryan branch 
of the Indo-European language family.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:36.360
And it's a descendant of the Sauraseni Prakrit language,
the language from which all modern Indo-Aryan 
languages descended from.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:41.240
And if we go back earlier than the Sauraseni Prakrit 
language, it is a descendant of Sanskrit.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:46.560
Early forms of Hindustani developed between 
the 7th to 13th century CE, a time of

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.520
heavy  Islamic influence on the Indian subcontinent, 
due to conquest by Central Asian Turkic envaders.

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:58.660
Then, in the 13th century CE, the Delhi Sultanate began 
its rule and expanded this Islamic influence.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.840
Hindustani was the language 
of the common people around Delhi,

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:06.920
while Persian became the official state language, 
and language of the courts and the language of the elite.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:12.920
The Delhi Sultanate was a Muslim kingdom which was 
ruled by successive Turkic and Afghan dynasties.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.000
These dynasties were Persianized, meaning that 
they looked up to and admired the Persian culture,

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.160
so they brought the Persian language
and its literary traditions with them to Hindustan.

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.860
And they also spoke their native languages, 
among them, Turkic languages.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.240
They were followed by the Mughal Empire in 1526.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:32.400
They were another Persianized people 
of Turkic-Mongol descent

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.580
and like the Delhi Sultanate, they introduced Persian 
as the official language of the Empire,

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:40.800
the lingua franca of the elite people
and the prestigious literary language.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.160
At the same time, Arabic was influencial
as a language of religion.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:50.760
During this timle, Hindustani, which was known by 
different names such as Hindavi and Delhavi,

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:57.200
spread over much of the northern Indian subcontinent 
as a lingua franca, althought there were some difference 
in vocabulary.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:04:02.700
The influence of Persian during these dynasties as well 
as the status of Arabic as the language of religion,

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:05.080
had an impact on the Hindustani language.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:10.680
But the degree of that impact depended on the local
area and the culture and the religion of the community.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:17.340
For examples, Muslim communities used more Arabic 
and Persian words and wrote in the Perso-Arabic script, called Nastaliq,

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.960
while Hindu communities used more sanskrit words 
and wrote in the Devanagari script.

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:27.560
In the 18th c., towards the end of the Mughal Empire, 
a form of Hindustani,

00:04:27.580 --> 00:04:32.840
based on the Khariboli dialect of Delhi, came to 
replace Persian as the language of the elite.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:37.540
And soon after, under British rule, it became 
the official language along with English.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.400
It was a variety of Hindustani, 
containing Persian vocabulary.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.120
Another name for that language started to be used, 
which was Urdu.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:50.700
That name Urdu is a shortened form of the Persian phrase 
"Zaban-e Urdu" which means "language of the camp".

00:04:50.860 --> 00:04:55.980
This is because the language was the lingua franca of the Mughal army and was used their army camps.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:00.400
Until this Persianized variety of Hindustani 
was made the official language,

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:05.340
Hindustani was considered a single language
for all communities, with mere local variations.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:10.200
But when the Urdu variety written in Nastaliq script 
was made an official language by the British,

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:16.160
this angered Hindus who thought that the language 
should be written in the Devanagari script,
which is native to the subcontinent.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.580
This erupted into a major dispute over which script 
the language should be written in.

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:25.700
Amidst this dispute, the standard language of 
Hindustani began to diverge into 2 different languages.

00:05:25.700 --> 00:05:32.820
With Hindi drawing on Sanskrit for much of its formal 
vocabulary and purging the language of some 
of its Persian and Arabic vocabulary.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:40.020
Urdu went the other direction, purging the language of 
some of its Sanskrit vocabulary and expanding 
the amount of Persain and Arabic vocabulary.

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:46.280
But this applies mainly to the standard written languages,
with the spoken languages remaining almost completely intelligible.

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:51.280
When a Hindi speaker and an Urdu speaker encounter
each other, they have no problem communicating at all.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:58.180
There might be some different vocabulary  that filters 
down from the literary language and there might be 
some different vocabulary coming from local dialects,

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:02.200
and there might be a slightly different accent 
but generally they are not different at all.

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:09.580
In fact, Bollywood movies are created in a kind of neutral 
Hindustani language, that is usually neither Hindi nor Urdu. It's both.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:13.940
To do this, they avoid using literary vocabulary 
that is specific to either language

00:06:13.940 --> 00:06:17.840
and they focus on the common vocabulary 
of the spoken languages.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:23.100
Let's look at a couple of sentences to see
how similar the 2 languages are in casual speech

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.660
and how they diverge in the more formal register.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:28.300
The 1st sentence means "I want to meet you"

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:30.260
First in casual Urdu.

00:06:32.980 --> 00:06:34.620
Now in casual Hindi

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:41.480
As you can see the casual forms are quite similar 
or almost the same.

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.180
Now in literary Urdu.

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:48.500
Now in literary Hindi.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:56.140
In the literary forms, you can see 
that the vocabulary is quite different

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.960
The next sentence: "I want to know how you are"

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:00.900
In casual Urdu.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:05.380
Now in casual Hindi.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:10.620
In literary Urdu.

00:07:14.860 --> 00:07:16.720
And now in literary Hindi.

00:07:21.160 --> 00:07:24.860
These examples basically show 
that Hindustani is a single language but that

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:31.700
literary Urdu and literary Hindi have been crafted to 
reflect the different religious and literary traditions
of their speakers.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:38.260
Of course, it would be a mistake to think that 
all Muslims on the Indian subcontinent speak Urdu
 and that all Hindu speak Hindi.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:41.800
But think of it like this: 
for people who specifically speak Hindustani,

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:45.880
their religion basically determines 
which one they speak, Hindi or Urdu.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:51.400
It would also be a mistake to think that Urdu is the 
Pakistani language while Hindi is the Indian language.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.540
Urdu is the official language of Pakistan,
along with English.

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:59.340
But in fact, only 8% of the population 
are native speakers of Urdu, as I said before.

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.420
Numerous other languages are spoken in Pakistan
and the most widely spoken one is actually Punjabi.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:07.900
But Urdu is the lingua franca 
and virtually everyone learns it.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:12.160
In India, Hindi and English are 
the official languages at the national level,

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.560
but each state is free to choose 
its own official languages too.

00:08:15.640 --> 00:08:20.620
There is a total of 22 recognized languages 
at the state level, and that includes Urdu

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.840
So Urdu is not just the language of Pakistan.

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:27.660
It's also an official language in 8 states in India, 
including the Delhi Capital region

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:31.460
It is spoken by 51.5 million native speakers in India

00:08:31.460 --> 00:08:35.860
And that is 3.5 times greater than the number 
of Urdu native speakers in Pakistan

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.580
So how hard is it to learn Hindustani? 
Either Hindi or Urdu.

00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:45.360
Well, if you're a speaker of an Indo-European language, 
then you already speak a language that's related to Hindustani.

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:51.480
So most of the grammatical concepts will be familiar to you 
but there will also be some challenging differences as well.

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:58.080
If we look back at our sentence from before,
in English, it tranlates as something like 
"I - you - meet - want"

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:02.600
English is a SVO language, 
while Hindustani is generally SOV

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:06.760
that means subject-object-verb 
and the verb goes at the end of the clause.

00:09:07.000 --> 00:09:10.080
You will also notice that "meet" comes also before "want".

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:16.120
So the auxiliary verb, or the helping verb, comes after 
the main verb at the very end of the clause or sentence.

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:21.220
In Hindi there are also feminine and masculine nouns
as well as three noun cases.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:26.540
But these 3 noun cases seem significantly simpler than 
some other languages that you may have encountered.

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:28.000
[cough] Hungarian

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:34.060
One of the hardest things about learning Hindi 
or Urdu just might be that so many people 
on the Indian subcontinent are fluent in English.

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:39.600
So it might be hard to get to speak any other language 
than English with you, especially highly educated people.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:45.000
But if you spend time with the common people 
that you meet every day, then you'll have plenty 
of chances to practice.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:50.060
Speaking about Hindi and Urdu can be confusing
because they used to be considered one language

00:09:50.060 --> 00:09:54.240
and now, in terms of spoken communication,
they are still essentially one language.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:10:00.360
Whether someone speaks Hindi or whether they speak 
Urdu depends more on their cultural and religious identity.

00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:04.720
Urdu speakers identify with islamic and Persian heritage
and its literay tradition,

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:10.400
while Hindi speakers identify with Sanskrit 
and Hindu religion and its literary tradition

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.520
They speak the same language, 
but, on top of that spoken language,

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:20.340
each group has adopted a formal literary tradition
that emphasizes its prefered cultural and religious identity.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.680
As a learner of the language, assuming that you want 
to converse and communicate with people, then

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:28.020
learning either variety will give you 
access to the other one.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:31.760
And that is a total number of 538 millions people

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:35.700
and with a diaspora all over the world,
even outside of the subcontinent

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:41.620
you will have countless opportunity to practice and
make new connections using the Hindustani language.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:43.520
And that brings us at the end of this video.

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:46.920
Speakers of Hindi or Urdu, we'd like
to hear your comments down below.

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:51.980
Tell us your experience with communicating with 
people who speaks the other variety of Hindustani.

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:56.120
Is it the same language to you? How much difference 
is there? How much similarity is there?

00:10:56.160 --> 00:10:57.600
Let us know in the comments.

